.TH RACKVM 1 "August 2026" "RackVM 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rackvm \- compact Linux/KVM virtual machine monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rackvm run
.I CONFIG
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B rackvm run --kernel
.I FILE
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B rackvm validate
.I CONFIG
.br
.B rackvm verify
.I CONFIG
.br
.B rackvm inspect
.I KERNEL
.br
.B rackvm devirtualise
.I CONFIG
.B --output
.I DIRECTORY
.br
.B rackvm doctor
.SH DESCRIPTION
RackVM boots an x86 Linux kernel and initramfs with hardware-assisted KVM execution. It provides multiple virtual CPUs, guest memory, an interrupt controller, a programmable timer, and an interactive serial console.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --kernel " FILE"
Use FILE as the Linux bzImage.
.TP
.BI --initrd " FILE"
Use FILE as the initial RAM filesystem.
.TP
.BI --name " NAME"
Set the virtual machine name.
.TP
.BI --memory " MIB"
Allocate between 64 and 65536 MiB of guest memory.
.TP
.BI --cpus " COUNT"
Create between one and 64 virtual CPUs, subject to the host limit.
.TP
.BI --cmdline " TEXT"
Set the Linux kernel command line.
.TP
.B --no-interactive
Disable serial-console input.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/kvm
Linux KVM device used for virtual machine execution.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. A nonzero value indicates invalid input, an unsupported host, or a virtual machine failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kvm (4),
.BR grub-mkconfig (8)
